Package: jellyqda
Type: Package
Title: 'RStudio'-Native, Fully Offline Coding of Interview Transcripts
Version: 0.9.4
Authors@R: person("Shelly", email = "jellyqda@systemli.org", role = c("aut", "cre"))
Description: Qualitative data analysis ('QDA') of interview transcripts,
    field notes and open survey answers on your own computer, completely
    offline. Supports deductive and inductive coding with codes and
    subcodes, coding guidelines (definition, anchor example, coding rule),
    memos, segments by speaker turns, the summarizing qualitative content
    analysis of Mayring (2014) <https://nbn-resolving.org/urn:nbn:de:0168-ssoar-395173>,
    word frequencies, keyword in context, case comparisons and code queries
    (AND, OR, NOT). Intercoder agreement between two coders is measured with
    Cohen's kappa (Cohen 1960) <doi:10.1177/001316446002000104>, Brennan and
    Prediger's kappa (Brennan and Prediger 1981) <doi:10.1177/001316448104100307>
    and Krippendorff's alpha (Hayes and Krippendorff 2007)
    <doi:10.1080/19312450709336664>, with a blind project copy and consensus
    coding. Graphics (code co-occurrence heatmap, code network, document
    portraits) are plain 'SVG'; projects can be exported in the 'REFI-QDA'
    exchange format for other 'QDA' software. Text is imported from plain
    text, 'Markdown', 'Word' and 'PDF' files or from survey tables ('CSV',
    'Excel') and frozen at import, so character offsets never drift. All
    project data lives in a single 'SQLite' file; every action is recorded in
    an audit trail and can be undone. A local 'shiny' app with an English and
    a German user interface and a scriptable API that returns tibbles share
    the same project file.
